<article class="article-body">

<p>A founder I worked with once estimated she was answering the same four or five questions every single week, different people, same questions, because the original decisions behind them had never been written down anywhere searchable. That's not a communication problem. It's a documentation gap, and it's one of the cheapest things to fix once you actually build the habit.</p>

<h2>What belongs in a decision log</h2>
<p>Not every choice needs a permanent record. The ones that do: anything that will plausibly get asked again, anything that affects more than one team, and anything where the reasoning matters as much as the outcome. "We're using Vendor A, not Vendor B, because of pricing and support response time" is worth logging. "I decided to grab coffee before the 10am" is not.</p>

<h2>Four fields, nothing more</h2>
<p>A decision log that's complicated to update stops getting updated. The version that actually survives: what was decided, why, who decided it, and when. No approval workflow, no mandatory template beyond those four fields. The lower the friction to add an entry, the more likely it is someone actually adds one in the moment instead of promising to "write it up later."</p>

<h2>Make it searchable, not just written</h2>
<p>A decision log buried in a folder nobody checks is functionally the same as no decision log. The value only shows up when someone can search it before asking the question out loud. That means it needs to live somewhere people already look, not a separate tool that becomes one more place to remember to check.</p>

<h2>Someone has to own keeping it current</h2>
<p>Decision logs decay the same way any system does without an owner. The habit that keeps it alive: whoever's in the room when a real decision gets made is responsible for logging it within the hour, not at the end of the day, not "when I get a chance." A log that's three weeks behind has already stopped being useful for the thing it's meant to prevent, the same question getting asked again.</p>

<h2>What changes once it's actually running</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Fewer repeated questions</strong>, because the answer is one search away instead of one more interruption on a founder's calendar.</li>
  <li><strong>Faster onboarding</strong>, because new hires can read the reasoning behind existing decisions instead of asking why things are the way they are.</li>
  <li><strong>Less re-litigating</strong>, because a decision with a documented rationale is harder to casually reopen than one everyone half-remembers differently.</li>
</ul>

